---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Paint Calculator Methodology and Assumptions | PaintPro Calculator"
  description="See the formulas, coverage ranges, deductions, texture adjustments, rounding rules, and limits used by the PaintPro Calculator."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-xl px-4 pt-8 pb-16 md:pt-14 md:pb-24">
    <article>
      <h1 class="mb-5 text-[2.5rem] md:mb-6 md:text-[3.75rem]">How the paint calculator works</h1>

      <div class="prose-linen [&>h2]:mt-12 [&>h2]:border-t [&>h2]:border-border [&>h2]:pt-7 md:[&>h2]:mt-14 md:[&>h2]:pt-8">
        <p class="text-[1.1875rem] leading-[1.55]">
          A room measurement can support a useful buying estimate, but it cannot predict exactly how paint will behave on your wall. The calculator shows a low, likely, and high amount so that uncertainty stays visible.
        </p>

        <h2>1. Measure the surfaces</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="space-y-3">
            <p><strong>Wall area:</strong> 2 × (length + width) × height</p>
            <p><strong>Ceiling area:</strong> length × width</p>
            <p>Door and window allowances are subtracted from wall area. The result never drops below zero.</p>
          </div>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 420 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="room-diagram-title room-diagram-desc" class="panel w-full max-w-[26rem] p-3">
            <title id="room-diagram-title">Room measurement diagram</title>
            <desc id="room-diagram-desc">A rectangular room labeled with length, width, and wall height.</desc>
            <g class="stroke-heading" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linejoin="round">
              <path d="M70 175 L280 175 L360 120 L150 120 Z" class="fill-light-gray" />
              <path d="M70 175 L70 65 L280 65 L280 175" class="fill-surface" />
              <path d="M280 175 L360 120 L360 25 L280 65" class="fill-border" />
            </g>
            <g class="stroke-accent" fill="none" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round">
              <path d="M90 195 L270 195" />
              <path d="M90 189 L90 201 M270 189 L270 201" />
              <path d="M295 190 L365 142" />
              <path d="M45 70 L45 170" />
              <path d="M39 70 L51 70 M39 170 L51 170" />
            </g>
            <g class="fill-heading font-serif italic" font-size="21">
              <text x="180" y="224" text-anchor="middle">length</text>
              <text x="351" y="188" text-anchor="middle">width</text>
              <text x="22" y="120" text-anchor="middle" transform="rotate(-90 22 120)">height</text>
            </g>
          </svg>
        </div>

        <h2>2. Apply coats and coverage</h2>
        <p>Paintable area is multiplied by the number of coats, then divided by an adjusted coverage rate. Until a label rate is supplied, the calculator uses these planning ranges:</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="table-ruled">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Selection</th>
                <th class="text-right">High coverage</th>
                <th class="text-right">Likely</th>
                <th class="text-right">Low coverage</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Budget</td><td class="text-right">400</td><td class="text-right">350</td><td class="text-right">300</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Standard</td><td class="text-right">450</td><td class="text-right">400</td><td class="text-right">350</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Premium</td><td class="text-right">500</td><td class="text-right">450</td><td class="text-right">400</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="callout text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed">Rates are square feet per gallon. These are planning assumptions, not promises about a product. Use the exact label or technical data sheet when available.</p>

        <h2>3. Adjust for the surface</h2>
        <p>The selected texture and condition reduce the working coverage rate. Smooth, previously painted walls use the base rate. Heavy texture, new drywall, stains, and glossy surfaces widen the practical uncertainty.</p>
        <p>These adjustments are intentionally visible in the detailed breakdown. They are approximations. A small test area is the best way to learn how a specific coating and surface work together.</p>

        <h2>4. Add a buffer and round for purchase</h2>
        <p>When selected, the calculator adds 15 percent after estimating the raw gallons. It then rounds each point in the range up to a whole gallon because that is the common purchase unit. The unrounded likely amount remains visible, so you can see when a rounded result is close to the next can.</p>

        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <div class="callout space-y-2">
          <p>A 20 × 20 foot room with 8 foot walls has 640 square feet of wall area.</p>
          <p>Two coats require 1,280 square feet of coverage.</p>
          <p>At the likely standard rate of 400 square feet per gallon, that is 3.20 gallons before the buffer.</p>
          <p>With a 15 percent buffer, it becomes 3.68 gallons, which rounds to a four-gallon purchase. The conservative end rounds to five gallons.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>What this calculator does not know</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>The exact coating, color change, tint base, roller nap, spray loss, or painter technique unless you account for it.</li>
          <li>Whether a wall needs repair, sealing, or a separate primer system.</li>
          <li>Whether two surfaces in the same estimate will use different products.</li>
          <li>Package sizes, return rules, or current prices at your store.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="pt-2 text-sm text-muted">Calculation version 1. Last reviewed July 11, 2026.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
